```

Wait, I see it now! Consider the case where `begin` is the last byte and it's a multi-byte char start. `next_code_point` returns `end`. Then `p = begin`. Then the loop reads `*p = *begin`. If `*begin` is NOT an align char, then `p == begin`, break. OK.

But consider the case where `begin` is the last byte and it's a multi-byte char start, and `next_code_point` returns `end`. Then `p = begin`. The loop reads `*p`. If `*begin` IS an align char... but wait, a multi-byte char start can't be an align char (align chars are `<`, `>`, `^`, `=` which are all ASCII < 0x80). So `*begin` (a multi-byte char start, >= 0x80) is not an align char. So `p == begin`, break. OK.

